WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.417
AI Radar helps researchers and students discover recent papers and understand
unfamiliar research. This walkthrough uses real, previously generated reports.

2
00:00:10.417 --> 00:00:20.208
Start with an official arXiv category. A specific code, such as C S dot A I,
searches the artificial intelligence subcategory.

3
00:00:20.208 --> 00:00:28.417
Here is a saved overview of five papers. The opening summary explains the shared
context before you explore each contribution.

4
00:00:28.417 --> 00:00:37.667
Each paper combines a plain language explanation with a concrete illustration.
This example explains how an agent can avoid tools that stop working.

5
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:47.625
Expand the card to inspect the problem, key idea, results, significance, and
limitations. Experimental conditions stay attached to the reported result.

6
00:00:47.625 --> 00:00:56.833
Source excerpts sit beside the explanation. You can inspect the quoted text and
its physical PDF page reference instead of trusting a summary alone.

7
00:00:56.833 --> 00:01:05.500
The top level deep reading button opens one compact list. Select one paper for a
close reading, or several for joint analysis.

8
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:12.208
Here we select the RATED paper. The selection remains editable after entering the
reading workspace.

9
00:01:12.208 --> 00:01:22.333
The paper arrives with its full text ready. Users can add their own PDFs, replace
a source, or remove a paper before explicitly generating a report.

10
00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:31.833
Now we switch to a separate, previously generated English joint reading example.
It compares two papers, totaling seventy five pages.

11
00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:40.250
The joint report first identifies the shared research question. Each paper keeps
its own title, source, and evidence references.

12
00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:49.208
Next, compare how the researchers investigated their questions, including their
interventions, monitoring methods, and evaluation strategies.

13
00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:58.167
The results section keeps evidence attached to individual papers. Different task
domains mean their accuracy numbers should not become a simple ranking.

14
00:01:58.167 --> 00:02:07.583
Expanding a citation reveals the original excerpt, the paper it belongs to, and
the PDF page. The source PDF is one click away.

15
00:02:07.583 --> 00:02:17.167
The report also explains connections and limitations. Automated checks help trace
the sources, but readers still need to verify scientific claims.

16
00:02:17.167 --> 00:02:24.792
Finally, joint takeaways connect what can be learned across the papers, while
keeping the limits of those connections visible.

17
00:02:24.792 --> 00:02:36.208
Our biggest lesson was turning a vague idea into clear requirements, then breaking
a large feature into smaller tasks. AI Radar connects discovery, understanding,
and source checking.
